+The above realization that the \enquote{break point property} also holds for $\{u\in\mathbb{R}^k:u_0=1\}$ and not just $\mathbb{S}^{k-1}$ like in Proposition~\ref{prop:minimum_width} motivates the question if the set $\mathcal{N}_k$ be characterized such that $m(\mathcal{N}_1)\ge\dots\ge m(\mathcal{N}_p)>\gamma=m(\mathcal{N}_{p+1})=\dots$ for some constant $\gamma$?
+
+\begin{itemize}
+    \item \cite{10.1145/1008731.1008736}
+    \item \cite{gritzmann_computational_1993}
+    \item \cite{ahn_minimum-width_2025}
+    \item \cite{ahn_k_2025}
+\end{itemize}
+
+The problem of determining the minimum width of a point cloud is well known in the computational geometry literature. More specifically, one looks for two parallel hyperplanes enclosing all points with smallest distance to each other. \cite{10.1145/323233.323234} gives algorithms for exact computation together with runtime complexity, but only for two and three dimensional point clouds.
